WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
France capped the work week at 35 hours to spread jobs, yet firms kept total hours largely unchanged through contract redesign. This is Unintended Consequences, Episode 91. In the spring of 2000, managers at a mid-sized automotive supplier near Leon sat down with union representatives to implement the new statutory 35-hour week. Instead of hiring additional permanent staff, the company expanded its use of temporary contracts

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:59.300
and reclassified many hours above 35 as overtime under newly negotiated annualized schedules. The policy had been written to reduce unemployment by sharing existing work. Within a few years, the same plant employed roughly the same number of total hours, but with a noticeably larger share of contingent workers whose wages and protections lagged behind those of the permanent core. The arithmetic was straightforward on paper. A 39-hour baseline reduced to 35,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:18.860
should have required roughly one additional worker for every nine already employed, if output stayed constant, yet the firm's records showed no such addition to the permanent roster. Instead, the extra capacity came from workers whose contracts reset each assignment, and whose benefits did not accumulate across assignments.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:45.980
The measure originated with the government of Leonel Jospin, elected in 1997 on a platform that included fighting France's stubbornly high unemployment rate, which stood near 12%. Labor Minister Martin Aubrey championed the reduction from the longstanding 39-hour statutory week. She drew on longstanding French debates about work-sharing that dated back to the 1980s and to earlier experiments in the midter-owned years.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:02:09.540
Policymakers believed that capping individual hours would force employers to spread the same volume of work across more people. This would lower joblessness without requiring large fiscal outlays. At the time, productivity growth remained solid, and several neighboring countries were also exploring shorter hours. The French approach therefore appeared both economically feasible and socially progressive.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:37.400
The government explicitly framed the change as a modernization of working life, rather than a blunt restriction. One objection that surfaced even then was that unemployment might be driven more by skill mismatches or regional imbalances than by total hours worked. The response was that a broad cap would still create openings at the margin because firms could not simply intensify the existing workforce indefinitely without visible productivity losses.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:59.240
The first Aubrey law of June 13, 1998 set the legal framework and offered financial incentives for firms that negotiated earlier reductions. The second law of January 19, 2000 made the 35-hour ceiling compulsory for firms with more than 20 employees, with smaller firms following in 2002.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:17.460
Early adopters in the public sector and in large manufacturing companies often paired the hour reduction with modest wage increases financed by productivity gains and state subsidies. Proponents pointed to initial surveys showing thousands of new hires in firms that had concluded collective agreements.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:31.420
Skeptics, including some employer organizations, warned that rigid hour caps would raise labor costs and encourage firms to shift work to overtime or to external contractors rather than expand permanent payrolls.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:43.260
The laws included explicit provisions for annualized time accounts precisely because negotiators recognized that seasonal or weekly variation in demand would otherwise make the cap unworkable.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:56.560
Those same accounts later became the primary vehicle for preserving total hours. Once the laws took full effect, many companies responded by redesigning compensation and staffing structures rather than expanding headcount.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:07.680
Annualized working time accounts allowed firms to schedule longer weeks during peak periods and shorter ones during slack periods while still averaging close to the old total hours.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:18.160
Over time exemptions were widened through branch level agreements. The use of fixed term contracts known as CDD and temporary agency workers rose sharply.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:34.440
By the mid-2000s, the share of temporary employment had increased several percentage points because contingent workers fell outside the core 35-hour calculation for many statistical purposes, measured average hours for permanent employees declined modestly,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:47.580
while overall labor input per firm remained relatively stable. Wage inequality widened between protected CDI staff and the growing pool of temporary workers who received lower hourly rates and fewer benefits.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:58.480
Second order effects appeared in work intensity. Remaining hours were often packed more densely with reports of accelerated production rhythms in both manufacturing and services.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:12.300
Unemployment did not fall by the margins originally projected. Estimates from INSE and OECD reviews placed the net employment effect in the low tens of thousands rather than the hundreds of thousands once hoped for.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:21.460
The policy therefore redistributed hours and protections more than it expanded total employment. Subsequent governments gradually loosened the framework.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:30.840
The 2007 Teppel law under Nicolas Sarcosi increased tax exemptions on overtime, explicitly encouraging hours above 35.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:39.420
Later reforms under Emanuel Macron further decentralized bargaining, giving individual firms more latitude to set working time rules.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:51.900
The statutory 35-hour ceiling itself was never repealed, yet its practical reach narrowed through accumulated exemptions and the expansion of forfeit jurors annualized packages for managerial staff.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:06:02.780
Today most full-time employees still reference the 35-hour benchmark in contracts, but actual average hours work to sit higher once over time and second jobs are included.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:09.100
The temporary contract share remains structurally elevated compared with the late 1990s.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:18.240
Rigid quantitative caps on a single input invite substitution across other margins, such as contract type, overtime rules and work intensity.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:26.480
When those margins are easier to adjust than the capped variable, the original objective can be met only partially or not at all.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:37.040
Policy makers therefore benefit from modeling the full menu of responses available to the organizations they regulate rather than assuming the capped variable will move in isolation.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:44.840
The French experience continues to surface whenever governments consider hard limits on working time or platform mediated labor.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:51.340
The question is not only what the rule prohibits, but which adjacent practices it leaves unrestricted.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:07:00.120
When a new rule limits contractor hours in the gig economy, which margin will platforms adjust first? And that's where today's story leaves us.

